Jon doubled over when he heard the news. The divorce was going to be final by August 5. This meant he lost the \textasciitilde{20} days that had overlapped with the divorce. The day before, Jon worked 180 minutes and during the days of separation, he worked an average of 4 hours. How many hours does Jon have to work in August to pay the $4,480 attorney fine?
\begin{frame}{\begin{itemize}[0pt]
\item As we know, the number of additional titles at the beginning of the process was three;
\item Jon has 180 minutes in August (which equals \\[4ex}
2 \textasciitilda{15} $\,.$);
\item There are 60 minutes in an hour;
\item So, the day before, Jon has to work an additional 180 minutes of overtime;
\item Since there are three day specified in the constraints, he works an additional four hours of overtime over those days;
\item Thus, he works a total of 8 hours of overtime;
\item Then, subtract the time he gets to spend with his family if he splits the legal costs with his ex-wife, which would maximise his earnings;
\item Celebrating all the time he had to spend with his friends;
\end{itemize}}

