Here, we are looking for the smallest positive integer $x$ that divides both $10^{-3378}$ and \((\sqrt[5]{6^5})^6\).

\begin{equation*}
10^{-3378} = 10^{(-5)(-3378)}, \qquad \frac{1}{(6^5)^603} = 6^5; \quad \text{and} \quad (6^5^6)^2 = 4^{1026}.
\end{equation*}

The largest integer that divides $10^{-3378}$ with base-2 \[matri"
c without generating overflow issues is $5$, which gives us the product $5 \times 1026 = 5100$. Thus, we know that $10^{5100}$ must be divisible by $15$ and $10000$.

